WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.528 --> 00:00:03.445
(woman vocalizing)

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:07.790
- [Chani] My planet
Arrakis is so beautiful

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:09.003
when the sun is low.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:16.045
Rolling over the sands, you
can see spice in the air.

00:00:16.045 --> 00:00:18.460
(dramatic music)

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:21.833
The outsiders ravaged our
lands in front of our eyes.

00:00:26.834 --> 00:00:29.920
(explosions blasting)
(men screaming)

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:33.350
Their cruelty to my
people is all I've known.

00:00:34.648 --> 00:00:37.554
(woman vocalizing)

00:00:37.554 --> 00:00:40.325
What's to become of our world?

00:00:40.325 --> 00:00:41.158
Paul.

00:00:41.158 --> 00:00:43.575
(Paul gasps)

00:00:45.994 --> 00:00:47.380
- [Duncan] My boy.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.090
- [Paul] Duncan, can I
trust you with something?

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:49.923
- Yes, always.

00:00:49.923 --> 00:00:50.756
You know that.

00:00:51.752 --> 00:00:55.960
- I've been having dreams
about a girl on Arrakis.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:57.690
I don't know what it means.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.820
- Dreams make good stories,

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.630
but everything important
happens when we're awake.

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:03.910
Hey, you put on some muscle?

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:05.070
- I did?

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:05.903
- No.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:09.200
- We are House Atreides.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.143
There is no call we do not answer.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.483
There is no faith that we betray.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.260
Smile, Gurney.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:18.550
- I am smiling.

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:21.830
- [Leto] The emperor asks us
to bring peace to Arrakis.

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:23.570
House Atreides accepts!

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:27.873
- I know you.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:31.570
There's something awakening in my mind.

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:36.030
- [Chani] You need to face your fears.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:39.943
- Come with me.

00:01:43.556 --> 00:01:44.613
- You need to be ready.

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:46.830
You've never met the Harkonnens before.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:47.663
They're not human.

00:01:47.663 --> 00:01:48.496
They're brutal!

00:01:50.668 --> 00:01:52.763
- [Wellington] The
duke's son sees too much.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.550
- [Vladimir] This is my dune.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:56.593
Kill them all.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.460
- God in heaven.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.020
(explosion blasting)

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:05.290
Get everything with guns off the ground!

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:06.123
Go!

00:02:08.324 --> 00:02:09.674
- This is an extermination.

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:13.680
They're picking my family off one by one.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:17.680
- Let's fight like demons.

00:02:25.451 --> 00:02:29.784
- Dad, what if I'm not the
future of House Atreides?

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:34.480
- A great man doesn't seek to lead.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:35.463
He's called to it.

00:02:38.183 --> 00:02:39.433
But if your answer is no,

00:02:41.433 --> 00:02:43.753
you'll still be the only
thing I ever needed you to be.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:46.743
My son.

00:02:46.743 --> 00:02:48.939
(woman vocalizing)

00:02:48.939 --> 00:02:50.606
If anything happens,

00:02:52.591 --> 00:02:53.837
will you protect Paul?

00:02:53.837 --> 00:02:55.087
- With my life.

00:02:59.274 --> 00:03:00.107
- No!

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:04.650
Only together can we stand a chance.

00:03:13.994 --> 00:03:14.994
- It's time.

00:03:16.447 --> 00:03:19.364
(woman vocalizing)

00:03:29.313 --> 00:03:32.030
- Hello, everyone, and
welcome to the Academy's

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:36.400
Visiting Artists Film Series
FilmAid edition for writing.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:37.360
I'm Vanessa Taylor,

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:40.240
and today I will be speaking
with the screenwriters of Dune,

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:43.190
Eric Roth, Jon Spaihts,
and Denis Villeneuve,

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:45.273
who also directed and produced the film.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.780
I am thrilled to be here today

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:01.020
to have the opportunity
to talk with you all

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:03.420
about this beautiful film,

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:07.173
and I will just dive in because
I have so many questions.

00:04:08.126 --> 00:04:09.820
I would like to start with you, Eric,

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:12.610
and I would love to know
what kind of dialogue

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:15.610
you and Denis had sort of going into this

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:17.893
as you approached your draft.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:22.180
- Well, we started kind of blind in a way,

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:24.320
I mean, except for, obviously, Denis.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:26.910
We both knew the book very well.

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:30.650
- And you had also, had you
also read it a long time ago?

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:32.020
I know, Denis, you had read it

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:33.660
when you were in your early teens.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.070
Eric, at what point had you--

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:36.870
- I had read it when I
was in my early teens.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:38.673
But then when Denis talked
to me about doing it,

00:04:38.673 --> 00:04:42.330
I obviously reread it
thoroughly, more than once

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:46.560
and wanted to see what were the pitfalls.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.870
And I also was asked to
write a long treatment

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:52.830
for the estate 'cause
I wanted to make sure

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.730
we weren't going to besmirch the piece

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:58.363
and take off on some kind of flyer.

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:04.380
But Denis is a wonderful partner

00:05:04.630 --> 00:05:08.010
and said, he gave me the right to dream.

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:08.960
And I take dreams

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.160
and I go a little bit
far afield sometimes.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:15.120
And he also, which we've
talked about a number of times,

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:16.950
I said, "What would be
most important to you?"

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:19.390
And he felt to somehow

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:24.327
show the women, the
importance of the women in it

00:05:24.327 --> 00:05:28.390
and make the piece have a
female quality, feminine quality

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:30.200
that would be in keeping with Sand Dune,

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:34.560
those kind of things, and
so that the Bene Gesserit

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:39.270
became as important in the
book and maybe more important

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:43.360
to what the whole sort
of theme of the movie is.

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:48.360
So, yeah, and I think we
tried to figure out a way

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:52.100
to tell a really, a resounding story,

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:54.610
which was given to us by Frank Herbert,

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:58.010
and not to go too far afield,

00:05:58.010 --> 00:06:00.750
but yet try in the most concise way.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:03.070
And I'm not the most concise writer,

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:04.730
so this was a challenge,

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:07.630
and he always had to have me
keep my feet on the ground.

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:12.590
And I will say, just to go
a slightly step further,

00:06:12.590 --> 00:06:13.870
and maybe this would be
something you talk about

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:17.340
down the line, this is, to
me, such a unique experience

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.970
because I've been involved with
other sort of writing teams

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:22.880
and sort of rewrote each
other and worked together.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:25.200
But this is the most
seamless that I can think of

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:27.550
where people worked to the
best of their abilities

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:30.250
on the things that they
knew how to do very well,

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:33.160
where I don't think you can
tell one from the other.

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:36.630
And so it's, I think, a
really unique thing to see.

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:39.683
And so I'm very proud of
that for everybody involved.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:42.470
- I'll go to you next, Denis.

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:46.160
I'm wondering what you were
looking for at this stage

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:47.593
in a collaborator.

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:49.813
- Oh, it's,

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:54.400
I asked Eric at the
beginning because I was,

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:59.400
I had read several of his previous work,

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:04.627
and I was always impressed
by the depth of his culture,

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:10.033
his reference, his link, his
relationship with spirituality.

00:07:13.136 --> 00:07:18.070
And also, it's true that
one thing that I love

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:23.070
in Eric's writing is how
we approached femininity.

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:24.820
I liked the idea that someone

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:28.517
who had the more of an
approach of an historian,

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:34.460
someone that will approach
this book with that view.

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:37.800
So that's why Eric was
the name I had in mind

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:41.163
to approach the beast, because frankly,

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:44.750
I don't know about Jon, on Jon's side,

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:49.750
but me, I was as excited
as terrorized, (laughing)

00:07:50.140 --> 00:07:53.280
and I needed someone with
wisdom and the experience

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:54.970
to crack it at the beginning.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:56.210
- Right.

00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:58.270
I would love, in a
moment, to go back, Eric,

00:07:58.270 --> 00:07:59.770
to what you said about pitfalls

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:02.420
and ask you all about what
those pitfalls might have been

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:04.040
that you were trying to avoid.

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:05.690
But first, I would love to know, Denis,

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:08.740
so you came in, I believe, after Eric

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:09.710
and were writing on this,

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:13.180
which you don't always do
on your directing projects.

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:17.610
And I just wondered
what you hoped to bring

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:19.713
as you began writing on this work.

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:25.100
- I started making movies by
writing my own feature films.

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:27.580
My first four feature films,

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:32.203
I was the unique screenwriter
on the four of them.

00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:36.210
The more I learned about writing,

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:40.240
the more I realized how little I know

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:45.240
and how much I needed to share
writing with other people.

00:08:45.507 --> 00:08:47.700
And my dream was to,

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:52.700
after having directed a few
movies written by other writers,

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:55.650
my dream for Dune was to share the writing

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:59.210
with other writers with more experience,

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:02.430
that have tremendously
more talent than me.

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:07.430
But I wanted to stay close to
the object of the adaptation.

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:12.173
I wanted to feel the
intimate, the intimacy,

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:17.220
to try to infuse

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:23.440
in Eric and in Jon visual
ideas that were haunting me.

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:29.780
My dream was to try to
bring back to the surface

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:34.780
images that I had dreamed
of when I read the book

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:36.360
when I was 13 years old.

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:39.310
And I wanted those images to,

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:42.223
to stay in relationship with
those images and try to,

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:49.390
I don't know, to suggest
them to Eric and Jon

00:09:49.390 --> 00:09:52.790
as we were, as the writing
was moving forward.

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:57.400
And Eric was very generous.

00:09:57.400 --> 00:10:01.570
The idea was, and I loved it,
it was like a paradise for me,

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:05.230
to take Eric's work, and
I had this benediction

00:10:06.853 --> 00:10:08.210
just to shape it to,

00:10:08.210 --> 00:10:10.270
not shaping, I apologize,
not the right word.

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:13.890
It's just that Eric will
agree that what he brought

00:10:16.741 --> 00:10:18.750
was a very impressive screenplay

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:23.053
but that was a bit too
ambitious for our resources.

00:10:23.053 --> 00:10:23.993
- Agreed, agreed.

00:10:24.861 --> 00:10:27.253
- And I had to add, with his benediction,

00:10:28.748 --> 00:10:32.330
I took the elements, not
reshape, but play with it.

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:33.590
- No, you did.

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:34.900
No, you did, and you did a,

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:38.570
I must say, I mean, I've worked
with probably 20 directors

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:41.970
and I was always, I'm always
sensitive when the directors

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:44.560
do what they, they either do
what they're supposed to do

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:46.950
as a director and edit,
do that kind of thing.

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:49.730
But Denis really did writing on it,

00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:51.560
I mean, pretty wonderful writing where he,

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:52.940
it was more than shaping, Denis.

00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:53.950
I think you--

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:55.350
- No, but it was tightening.

00:10:57.810 --> 00:11:02.490
Thank you, Eric, but it
was, for me, so inspiring

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:05.610
to see Eric's take on the material,

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:09.140
and it's something that
resonated to the old process.

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:09.973
It was,

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:17.410
but the deal was that
I had Eric that draft

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:18.780
and then I was working.

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:22.393
And then, of course, I needed help,

00:11:23.310 --> 00:11:28.310
and Jon came and did a
tremendous amount of work to,

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:33.737
again, dig into, more into characters.

00:11:33.737 --> 00:11:36.413
And it was like, it needed, for me,

00:11:39.914 --> 00:11:40.930
and I had felt that since the beginning,

00:11:40.930 --> 00:11:42.960
that we will need to be a lot of brains

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:47.960
in order to crack the novel,
and a team, that was the idea.

00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:50.420
There's a lot of movies
that I love in my life

00:11:50.420 --> 00:11:53.104
where when I was looking
at the credits at the end,

00:11:53.104 --> 00:11:55.920
there were like five
screenwriters that I admire.

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:58.050
And I was like, I was dreaming

00:11:58.050 --> 00:12:01.020
to have that kind of
relationship with the keyboard,

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:03.460
to have like collegiality.

00:12:03.460 --> 00:12:05.160
And that's the thing I'm grateful,

00:12:07.818 --> 00:12:10.120
that it was that case for Dune.

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:12.080
I'm really, it was a privilege for me

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:14.693
to work with both of these gentlemen.

00:12:15.656 --> 00:12:19.290
- And you said that when
you were approaching Eric,

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:21.940
you were looking at him
almost as a historian

00:12:21.940 --> 00:12:25.430
and talked about the other
qualities he brought to this.

00:12:25.430 --> 00:12:28.940
What were you looking for
from Jon as a collaborator?

00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:31.810
- Jon is, was, first of all,

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:36.810
the precision of his
writing, very impressive.

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:41.360
And there's something Jon
also, as deep as Eric does,

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:44.600
but he has a deep culture of the book.

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:47.960
He knows the book more than
anybody else, upside down.

00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:51.170
He can recite passage of the book.

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:53.020
It's like if he had participate

00:12:53.020 --> 00:12:54.510
to the writing of the book himself.

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:57.040
And there was something
about that deep knowledge

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:01.393
of the book that I needed
to bring on board and also,

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:09.390
to that link with areas
that I was looking for

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:14.360
and to bring the romanticism also.

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:17.540
It was like, I think that bringing in Jon,

00:13:17.540 --> 00:13:21.583
it brought a kind of equilibrium
with all that Eric wrote,

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:25.150
my work, and Jon, it brings something

00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:27.433
that had the proper equilibrium.

00:13:27.433 --> 00:13:28.266
That's what I would say.

00:13:28.266 --> 00:13:31.810
- I used to always say that
he put the movie on its feet.

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:32.643
I mean, it was,

00:13:33.605 --> 00:13:35.623
or I'm not saying he
brought it down to earth,

00:13:35.623 --> 00:13:36.870
(Denis laughs)

00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:40.104
but in other words, he was able
to accomplish both in a way.

00:13:40.104 --> 00:13:42.210
And he made one of the great, I think,

00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:44.640
wonderful transformations,

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:49.100
making one character a
black woman that was a man,

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:51.893
and I think that was sort
of an astounding idea.

00:13:51.893 --> 00:13:56.117
But when I read his draft,
you're always very suspect.

00:13:56.117 --> 00:13:57.469
What happened here?

00:13:57.469 --> 00:13:58.302
- [Vanessa] Right.

00:13:58.302 --> 00:13:59.135
- Where did my work go, or something?

00:13:59.135 --> 00:13:59.968
- Right.

00:13:59.968 --> 00:14:01.827
- I didn't feel that way because
I think he, first of all,

00:14:01.827 --> 00:14:04.580
was so true to the book in the right ways

00:14:04.580 --> 00:14:07.267
and understood what Denis wanted.

00:14:07.267 --> 00:14:09.970
And he did such yeoman's work.

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:12.440
I mean, it's really an incredible task

00:14:12.440 --> 00:14:16.196
that I think he did, and my
hat's completely off to him.

00:14:16.196 --> 00:14:17.420
- So, sorry.
- Yeah.

00:14:17.420 --> 00:14:19.530
I'm just gonna say, I mean, I'm such,

00:14:19.530 --> 00:14:21.960
I'm just a more fanciful
writer than Jon is.

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:24.860
And that doesn't mean better, but I just,

00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:26.220
I take flights of fancy.

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:28.210
And I won't say what we did,

00:14:28.210 --> 00:14:30.997
but when I first wrote
something and then he said,

00:14:30.997 --> 00:14:31.830
"That's lovely.

00:14:31.830 --> 00:14:33.689
"And how are we gonna afford
the rest of the movie?"

00:14:33.689 --> 00:14:36.068
(group laughs)

00:14:36.068 --> 00:14:36.901
And that was true.

00:14:36.901 --> 00:14:39.200
But I think, as I said, I
think Jon really gave it

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:44.213
the foundation for it that
makes the movie stand up.

00:14:45.913 --> 00:14:48.700
- So Jon, I would love to
hear from you then about,

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:49.790
first of all, it sounds like

00:14:49.790 --> 00:14:51.620
you had a real history with this book.

00:14:51.620 --> 00:14:55.010
Second of all, I cannot even
imagine what it would be like

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:58.600
to know that you were going
to come into this process

00:14:58.600 --> 00:15:01.660
after both Eric and Denis
had been working in it.

00:15:01.660 --> 00:15:06.660
Were you, I mean, did it feel crazy to be,

00:15:07.070 --> 00:15:11.180
I also wonder, I didn't
ask you others about this,

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:15.520
but it's so famous and gigantic, Dune.

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:18.863
I mean, was there, for any of
you, a sense of intimidation

00:15:18.863 --> 00:15:21.490
(chuckles) in the face of
this material, or not so much?

00:15:21.490 --> 00:15:23.190
It sounds like, Jon,
you're really knowledgeable

00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:25.401
about that material.

00:15:25.401 --> 00:15:26.590
- I'm sorry, Jon.

00:15:26.590 --> 00:15:29.617
I would just say, me, I was
afraid at the beginning.

00:15:29.617 --> 00:15:32.270
And I remember having
a discussion with Eric

00:15:32.270 --> 00:15:36.370
at the beginning, and
I had been so grateful

00:15:36.370 --> 00:15:40.470
that Eric took the baton at the beginning

00:15:40.470 --> 00:15:44.600
and start to really try to tame the beast

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:48.240
because I was impressed.

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:51.500
I was like a bit paralyzed
at the beginning, I remember.

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:53.000
That's what I wanted, on my side.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:53.833
- Yeah.

00:15:55.092 --> 00:15:55.925
Jon?

00:15:56.833 --> 00:15:57.900
- Well, I did know the book very well.

00:15:57.900 --> 00:16:00.520
I read it fairly young at 12 or 13,

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:02.030
and it was one of those books I've reread

00:16:02.030 --> 00:16:04.370
every summer vacation for
many years growing up,

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:06.305
so it was an old friend.

00:16:06.305 --> 00:16:07.655
- Wow.

00:16:07.655 --> 00:16:09.050
- And I'd put it aside for a long time,

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:11.300
but it was shocking when I went back to it

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:13.895
to realize how well I still knew it.

00:16:13.895 --> 00:16:17.210
I think there's something about
the plasticity of the mind

00:16:17.210 --> 00:16:19.121
in your youth, and the book--

00:16:19.121 --> 00:16:21.040
- Was that exciting or intimidating,

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:24.770
that this was such a beloved book for you?

00:16:24.770 --> 00:16:25.880
Or both?
- For me, honestly,

00:16:25.880 --> 00:16:26.950
it was pure excitement.

00:16:26.950 --> 00:16:30.860
I mean, it was exciting to
be rubbing elbows with giants

00:16:30.860 --> 00:16:32.610
like Eric and Denis.

00:16:32.610 --> 00:16:36.170
It's always exciting to,
just to be in the company

00:16:36.170 --> 00:16:40.083
of people with such bodies
of work and such stature.

00:16:42.233 --> 00:16:45.760
And yet, I was not nervous about it.

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:47.570
There were challenges to solve,

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:51.680
but the biggest one had been
solved before I came aboard

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:56.200
by Denis' decision to tackle
the novel in two films.

00:16:56.200 --> 00:16:57.620
- [Vanessa] Right.

00:16:57.620 --> 00:17:01.980
- There is no way to fit
Dune into a single film

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:05.660
even at a Lawrence of Arabia running time

00:17:05.660 --> 00:17:10.490
without dire surgery,
without a level of editing

00:17:10.490 --> 00:17:13.120
that would amount to amputation.

00:17:13.120 --> 00:17:14.820
But in two films,

00:17:14.820 --> 00:17:18.030
I think you can get to
the pith of the story.

00:17:18.030 --> 00:17:20.640
There's been a lot of talk over the years

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:24.080
about Dune as some sort
of uncrackable story or

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:28.420
unadaptable book.

00:17:28.420 --> 00:17:30.403
I've never seen it, honestly.

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:35.800
A couple of storied filmmakers
have taken runs at it.

00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:37.470
I think all of those storied filmmakers

00:17:37.470 --> 00:17:39.960
were less than perfectly
suited to the material

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:42.400
despite their extraordinary gifts,

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:44.510
and there have been
fountains of creativity

00:17:44.510 --> 00:17:45.733
around those efforts.

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:51.690
But Dune as a cinematic
story is not broken.

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:54.290
There are things that I am afraid to adapt

00:17:54.290 --> 00:17:56.640
or that I think are nearly unadaptable.

00:17:56.640 --> 00:17:58.450
There's a wonderful sci-fi novel

00:17:58.450 --> 00:18:00.050
called The Stars My Destination

00:18:00.050 --> 00:18:02.670
that's been kicking around
Hollywood for decades,

00:18:02.670 --> 00:18:03.810
ever since it was written.

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:05.250
That one might be unadaptable.

00:18:05.250 --> 00:18:07.360
Some things nearly are.

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:09.170
But Dune was ready to be a film,

00:18:09.170 --> 00:18:10.380
especially in the present day

00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:11.720
when the technology of filmmaking

00:18:11.720 --> 00:18:14.020
has caught up to the scope of the world.

00:18:14.020 --> 00:18:16.150
So for me, honestly, I was excited.

00:18:16.150 --> 00:18:17.590
I wanted to roll up my sleeves.

00:18:17.590 --> 00:18:19.150
I was ready to jump in

00:18:19.150 --> 00:18:23.900
and deeply reassured by
the quality of my partners.

00:18:23.900 --> 00:18:25.466
- Yes.

00:18:25.466 --> 00:18:26.299
And--
- Is there books,

00:18:26.299 --> 00:18:27.143
sorry, is there books,

00:18:27.143 --> 00:18:29.700
I would be curious to know, you, Eric,

00:18:29.700 --> 00:18:32.333
is there books that you
think that are unadaptable?

00:18:33.544 --> 00:18:34.544
- Adaptable?

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:38.580
Well, the only thing,
I wouldn't say a book.

00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:39.970
This was, Stanley Kubrick said

00:18:39.970 --> 00:18:42.924
you could not make a
movie about the Holocaust

00:18:42.924 --> 00:18:44.300
- Oh.
- Yeah.

00:18:44.300 --> 00:18:46.573
He said that's untouchable.

00:18:49.590 --> 00:18:52.010
I could say something
nasty about another movie,

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:55.680
but I won't, attempted the Holocaust.

00:18:55.680 --> 00:18:58.330
But I'm trying to think.

00:18:58.330 --> 00:19:01.940
Maybe if, I don't know, maybe the Bible.

00:19:01.940 --> 00:19:03.300
I don't know, I don't know.

00:19:03.300 --> 00:19:04.887
They've tried and it's what it is.

00:19:04.887 --> 00:19:06.250
- Right.
- I don't know.

00:19:06.250 --> 00:19:08.150
That's a great question.

00:19:08.150 --> 00:19:11.050
I can name other books that
they say that you can't do,

00:19:11.050 --> 00:19:14.450
maybe A Thousand Years of Solitude.

00:19:14.450 --> 00:19:15.830
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:19:15.830 --> 00:19:18.491
In that's case, it's not easy to do--

00:19:18.491 --> 00:19:21.330
- Yeah, I know, it would go on and on.

00:19:21.330 --> 00:19:24.270
- So I do wanna go back to a couple things

00:19:24.270 --> 00:19:27.000
you guys touched on, but I
first wanted to pick up with Jon

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:29.810
that I think what Denis
must have been referring to

00:19:29.810 --> 00:19:31.250
was Liet-Kynes.

00:19:31.250 --> 00:19:32.520
Is that correct?

00:19:32.520 --> 00:19:33.353
- That's right.

00:19:33.353 --> 00:19:35.010
- And why was it your impulse

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:37.943
to make that shift that he referred to?

00:19:40.060 --> 00:19:43.190
- We were looking broadly for

00:19:44.900 --> 00:19:46.550
justified and organic ways

00:19:46.550 --> 00:19:49.660
to strengthen the female
presence in the film.

00:19:49.660 --> 00:19:51.740
Dune as a novel has some very powerful

00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:53.390
and memorable female characters,

00:19:53.390 --> 00:19:55.113
really seminal female characters,

00:19:56.660 --> 00:19:59.257
but it's still a mostly male book,

00:19:59.257 --> 00:20:01.523
and in some ways, a product of its time.

00:20:02.550 --> 00:20:04.130
So we were looking for opportunities.

00:20:04.130 --> 00:20:06.410
And the character of Liet-Kynes

00:20:06.410 --> 00:20:09.520
is a little peculiar in several ways.

00:20:09.520 --> 00:20:14.520
In the very earliest
notebook drafts of Dune,

00:20:14.570 --> 00:20:16.730
Liet-Kynes was the protagonist.

00:20:16.730 --> 00:20:21.730
And the, when Herbert was
first brainstorming the book,

00:20:21.890 --> 00:20:24.780
and this Imperial ecologist
trying to solve the riddle

00:20:24.780 --> 00:20:28.370
of this impossible ecosystem
and to tame a planet

00:20:28.370 --> 00:20:31.463
without killing what made
it special, he was the hero.

00:20:32.704 --> 00:20:34.070
And then over time, he found his way.

00:20:34.070 --> 00:20:35.870
I don't know for how
long that was the case,

00:20:35.870 --> 00:20:37.620
but certainly, in early notebook notes,

00:20:37.620 --> 00:20:40.120
Liet-Kynes was the protagonist,

00:20:40.120 --> 00:20:43.750
and then he found his way to
the story of the Atreides clan.

00:20:43.750 --> 00:20:46.800
And so there are ways in which

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:50.630
Liet fits a little awkwardly in the story.

00:20:50.630 --> 00:20:51.463
There's a,

00:20:52.650 --> 00:20:54.220
spoilers for a moment.

00:20:54.220 --> 00:20:58.570
There's a relationship in the
novel that bears no fruit.

00:20:58.570 --> 00:21:01.320
We find out that Liet-Kynes

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:04.467
is actually the father of
Chani among the Fremen,

00:21:06.500 --> 00:21:09.230
which is very awkward in the storytelling

00:21:09.230 --> 00:21:13.750
because then, Chani finds
out that Liet-Kynes died

00:21:13.750 --> 00:21:16.280
right before she and Paul
are kind of falling in love.

00:21:16.280 --> 00:21:18.890
And they have to process
dad dying very quickly

00:21:18.890 --> 00:21:20.590
and then pivot to falling in love.

00:21:21.650 --> 00:21:22.760
And then we never really talk about

00:21:22.760 --> 00:21:24.220
that relationship again in the story.

00:21:24.220 --> 00:21:26.513
It's kind of a phantom limb.

00:21:27.730 --> 00:21:28.640
And there was certainly,

00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:30.840
it would only be confusing
to try to introduce

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:33.380
and then sweep away that relationship

00:21:33.380 --> 00:21:35.203
in the narrow confines of a film.

00:21:36.894 --> 00:21:40.370
So Liet-Kynes' only relationship
that was left in our book

00:21:40.370 --> 00:21:43.170
is really a relationship
to the planet of Arrakis,

00:21:43.170 --> 00:21:48.170
to the ecosystem, and to the
desert tribes of the Fremen

00:21:49.520 --> 00:21:51.840
with whom Liet has completely integrated.

00:21:51.840 --> 00:21:56.840
And the message Liet brings
is so fundamentally maternal

00:21:57.240 --> 00:22:02.240
and about healing a world,
and preserving the people,

00:22:02.370 --> 00:22:07.370
and finding a way of integrating
the aboriginal tribes,

00:22:08.130 --> 00:22:11.060
which were so much a part of her life now,

00:22:11.060 --> 00:22:14.670
and this miraculous ecosystem
that's evolved on this planet

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:19.410
that to make her a woman almost
felt obvious after the fact,

00:22:19.410 --> 00:22:20.243
that it almost,

00:22:20.243 --> 00:22:22.183
as if she should have
been from the beginning.

00:22:23.952 --> 00:22:25.170
And of course, this is the human diaspora

00:22:25.170 --> 00:22:27.913
10,000 years in our future or more,

00:22:29.090 --> 00:22:33.750
so anything that we would
read in a racial text

00:22:33.750 --> 00:22:35.093
should no longer apply.

00:22:36.241 --> 00:22:39.170
It's not a race blind universe,

00:22:39.170 --> 00:22:41.700
but certainly, what we perceive as race

00:22:41.700 --> 00:22:44.880
should have moved beyond
any envelope we have for it,

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:47.230
and people are identifying
much more strongly

00:22:47.230 --> 00:22:48.970
with their planets of origin,

00:22:48.970 --> 00:22:51.760
with the noble houses that govern them,

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:55.213
with societies and orders
they belong to, and so forth.

00:22:56.281 --> 00:22:59.890
- And I think that this idea
of changing the gender of Kynes

00:22:59.890 --> 00:23:02.960
was absolutely a brilliant
idea that Jim came,

00:23:02.960 --> 00:23:05.073
sorry, Jon came with.

00:23:06.089 --> 00:23:07.700
And I was very excited by this idea

00:23:07.700 --> 00:23:09.390
because it happened at a moment

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:11.630
where I was feeling a disequilibrium,

00:23:11.630 --> 00:23:14.110
because Eric had wrote things

00:23:14.110 --> 00:23:15.370
that I was deeply in love with,

00:23:15.370 --> 00:23:20.370
which was that he had studied
the past of Lady Jessica,

00:23:21.800 --> 00:23:23.740
her training as a Bene Gesserit.

00:23:23.740 --> 00:23:26.420
All that feminine strength
and that feminine training

00:23:26.420 --> 00:23:28.700
was in the earlier draft,

00:23:28.700 --> 00:23:33.700
and for reasons of
storytelling, I had to remove,

00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:35.370
but I always felt the pain

00:23:35.370 --> 00:23:38.613
of removing those beautiful
things that Eric had done,

00:23:39.690 --> 00:23:43.203
and still, today, I'm dreaming
to shoot that one in my life.

00:23:44.949 --> 00:23:47.663
But that disequilibrium
brought this desire

00:23:50.120 --> 00:23:51.580
to find femininity somewhere else,

00:23:51.580 --> 00:23:54.173
and Jon came with this idea.

00:23:56.430 --> 00:23:58.240
- I'd love to go back just for a moment

00:23:58.240 --> 00:24:01.140
for each of you to origins in the sense of

00:24:01.140 --> 00:24:03.710
I understand that you each
had a connection with the book

00:24:03.710 --> 00:24:04.570
at a young age.

00:24:04.570 --> 00:24:08.053
But what would you say for you,

00:24:09.250 --> 00:24:10.433
what is essential?

00:24:11.620 --> 00:24:15.380
In terms of guiding principles,
what needed to be preserved?

00:24:15.380 --> 00:24:17.680
What is it about this piece of work?

00:24:17.680 --> 00:24:20.070
What is this fundamentally about

00:24:20.070 --> 00:24:21.713
that was so meaningful to you

00:24:24.330 --> 00:24:26.670
that you felt this longing to do it?

00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:28.740
And was it the same for each of you?

00:24:28.740 --> 00:24:31.070
Would you each tell me
that the book and the movie

00:24:31.070 --> 00:24:32.190
are about the same things?

00:24:32.190 --> 00:24:34.993
Or would you each perhaps
have a different answer?

00:24:35.912 --> 00:24:37.390
- I will say that I suspect

00:24:37.390 --> 00:24:38.840
that we have all different answers

00:24:38.840 --> 00:24:41.273
because the beauty of this book is,

00:24:42.578 --> 00:24:43.740
and that's why I think it's a masterpiece,

00:24:43.740 --> 00:24:46.190
is that it has so many
layers that you discover

00:24:46.190 --> 00:24:47.850
according to your own life experience.

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:50.330
When I read it at 13
years old, I didn't see,

00:24:50.330 --> 00:24:55.330
saw the same thing when
I read it again at 20

00:24:55.430 --> 00:24:57.220
and again at 30 something.

00:24:57.220 --> 00:25:02.220
It's a book that conveys
so many layers and ideas.

00:25:02.480 --> 00:25:07.223
For me, one of the most
important idea was the one that,

00:25:09.710 --> 00:25:12.730
a specific school, the
Bene Gesserit sisters

00:25:12.730 --> 00:25:16.140
are able to channel ancient voice,

00:25:16.140 --> 00:25:18.550
a voice coming from their genetic past,

00:25:18.550 --> 00:25:21.980
it's a voice from their
ancestors, and to take lessons.

00:25:21.980 --> 00:25:24.540
Instead of transforming those voices,

00:25:24.540 --> 00:25:26.580
those ghosts into a neurosis,

00:25:26.580 --> 00:25:30.050
they are using them as a tool to,

00:25:30.050 --> 00:25:31.410
and transform it into a power.

00:25:31.410 --> 00:25:34.010
And that, for me, is an idea that,

00:25:34.010 --> 00:25:37.510
it really deeply spoke to me
and that I wanted to make sure

00:25:37.510 --> 00:25:40.583
that it would be a
prominent in the adaptation.

00:25:41.937 --> 00:25:43.120
- What about for you, Eric?

00:25:43.120 --> 00:25:47.330
- Well, the book was so
part and parcel of my,

00:25:47.330 --> 00:25:50.460
particularly growing up, the culture.

00:25:50.460 --> 00:25:52.240
I wouldn't say, and just to be honest,

00:25:52.240 --> 00:25:55.093
the book was my favorite
book I've ever read.

00:25:56.130 --> 00:25:59.160
It didn't impact me quite that way.

00:25:59.160 --> 00:26:01.530
I mean, I thought it was astounding,

00:26:01.530 --> 00:26:04.560
and the work he did was
prodigious and creating languages,

00:26:04.560 --> 00:26:07.220
and obviously, the
formation of this planet

00:26:07.220 --> 00:26:09.640
and all the prescient
things we learned today.

00:26:09.640 --> 00:26:12.870
But I mean, Earth Day began in like 1968.

00:26:12.870 --> 00:26:15.672
So he was still ahead of that but--

00:26:15.672 --> 00:26:16.505
- Right.

00:26:16.505 --> 00:26:17.338
- People were very aware

00:26:17.338 --> 00:26:19.253
of kind of the loss of
water and everything.

00:26:20.380 --> 00:26:23.700
But as you know, from
knowing me as well as you do

00:26:23.700 --> 00:26:25.550
since you were a junior high student,

00:26:27.732 --> 00:26:30.987
I was kind of a hippie, and
this book reflected my era

00:26:31.890 --> 00:26:33.240
in many, many ways.

00:26:33.240 --> 00:26:36.480
We don't have to talk about
drugs so much, but that,

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:40.670
the point of view of varying societies

00:26:40.670 --> 00:26:42.780
kind of clashing in some ways,

00:26:42.780 --> 00:26:44.750
what is the sense of violence,

00:26:44.750 --> 00:26:48.353
what is the beauty femininity,

00:26:49.440 --> 00:26:52.740
as we talked about, dreams,
I mean, all of these things,

00:26:52.740 --> 00:26:56.630
the prescient nature of the
boy who now has this kind of,

00:26:56.630 --> 00:26:59.170
which, I mean, was, I think
that was personal to me,

00:26:59.170 --> 00:27:00.640
I guess I'd say about,

00:27:00.640 --> 00:27:03.700
not that I'm a prophet, but the idea

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:06.980
of what is it coming to
manhood and then what are your,

00:27:06.980 --> 00:27:09.600
what do you have to then accomplish

00:27:09.600 --> 00:27:13.399
when you set out after that
and what is that next journey.

00:27:13.399 --> 00:27:14.232
- [Vanessa] Right.

00:27:14.232 --> 00:27:15.283
- Yeah, so I mean, it was,

00:27:16.130 --> 00:27:18.690
for me, it wasn't this was a job,

00:27:18.690 --> 00:27:21.190
but it was, I think, an
astounding job because of,

00:27:22.128 --> 00:27:24.320
it's also the history of the piece

00:27:24.320 --> 00:27:25.980
with some great people trying it,

00:27:25.980 --> 00:27:30.980
and then this whole challenge
of what can I bring to this.

00:27:31.250 --> 00:27:33.020
And so anyway, so I think it's just,

00:27:33.020 --> 00:27:35.100
I think they're both right, Jon and Denis,

00:27:35.100 --> 00:27:38.313
that I think it's a prism
from where you came from.

00:27:39.976 --> 00:27:42.330
And I sort of gravitated to the family,

00:27:42.330 --> 00:27:44.290
which I have a big family,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:27:44.290 --> 00:27:46.570
And as I say, this kind of,

00:27:46.570 --> 00:27:49.220
my favorite movie was 2001,

00:27:49.220 --> 00:27:51.970
so that was kind of a
different point of view

00:27:51.970 --> 00:27:54.703
of mortality, if you will.

00:27:56.010 --> 00:27:57.873
- And Jon, what is it about for you?

00:28:00.850 --> 00:28:02.123
- There's so much in it,

00:28:03.172 --> 00:28:07.250
and you can go on any
number of deep dives.

00:28:07.250 --> 00:28:10.270
And Denis is right that
it's also different books

00:28:10.270 --> 00:28:11.143
at different times.

00:28:11.143 --> 00:28:13.610
It's very different reading it as a boy

00:28:13.610 --> 00:28:16.700
about Paul Atreides's age
than it is reading it now

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:18.650
as the father of an eight-year-old son,

00:28:19.494 --> 00:28:20.900
and I definitely attach

00:28:20.900 --> 00:28:23.330
to different points in the narrative now.

00:28:23.330 --> 00:28:28.330
But for me, the real soul of
Dune is almost an abstraction.

00:28:28.430 --> 00:28:32.400
If you read most genre fantasy,
you tend to find characters

00:28:32.400 --> 00:28:35.433
representing clear and
bold points of view,

00:28:35.433 --> 00:28:39.163
for the king, for honor,
for family, whatever it is.

00:28:40.750 --> 00:28:45.750
And in Dune, every
character has this nuanced

00:28:45.830 --> 00:28:50.830
and many layered appreciation
of their circumstance

00:28:51.280 --> 00:28:52.730
and their place in the world.

00:28:54.570 --> 00:28:56.740
Duke Leto Atreides has a code of honor,

00:28:56.740 --> 00:28:59.250
and he believes in it.

00:28:59.250 --> 00:29:01.440
He has a family that he loves dearly.

00:29:01.440 --> 00:29:06.060
He is aware of the Imperium as
a morally dubious enterprise

00:29:06.060 --> 00:29:08.470
founded on physical strength

00:29:08.470 --> 00:29:10.360
and the doctrine of might makes right.

00:29:10.360 --> 00:29:12.963
He knows that there are
tyrants abroad in the land.

00:29:15.235 --> 00:29:16.068
He is honorable

00:29:16.068 --> 00:29:20.110
and is aware that his
performance of honor is theater,

00:29:20.110 --> 00:29:22.810
and that it is good propaganda,
and that it lends him power.

00:29:22.810 --> 00:29:24.350
It attracts him followers.

00:29:24.350 --> 00:29:25.970
He's aware that it's still honorable

00:29:25.970 --> 00:29:27.280
and that that's a good thing.

00:29:27.280 --> 00:29:29.130
He knows that this will all end someday

00:29:29.130 --> 00:29:31.083
that he is not always,

00:29:32.640 --> 00:29:35.580
not in always the good guy
in any one interaction.

00:29:35.580 --> 00:29:38.730
They have a very real world grasp

00:29:38.730 --> 00:29:41.430
of the complexity of their positions.

00:29:41.430 --> 00:29:43.660
They are trying to be good people.

00:29:43.660 --> 00:29:46.500
They are aware that they are participating

00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:50.040
in systems that are imperfect

00:29:50.040 --> 00:29:52.270
and systems that have
darkness within them.

00:29:52.270 --> 00:29:54.160
They try to steer in the best directions.

00:29:54.160 --> 00:29:57.530
To some extent they
embrace a certain fatalism

00:29:57.530 --> 00:29:59.110
that this is what they were born to,

00:29:59.110 --> 00:30:01.890
that the system is beyond
their power to change.

00:30:01.890 --> 00:30:05.443
But at every level of the book,
Jessica as a Bene Gesserit,

00:30:06.380 --> 00:30:11.380
Stilgar as a Fremen, Paul and
Leto Atreides as noblemen,

00:30:12.660 --> 00:30:16.460
rulers of a planet, rivals
of other great houses,

00:30:16.460 --> 00:30:19.420
all of them appreciate the
complexity of their position

00:30:19.420 --> 00:30:21.210
and will, in private moments,

00:30:21.210 --> 00:30:23.660
philosophize with each other about that.

00:30:23.660 --> 00:30:27.510
It felt, in that way, like real life.

00:30:27.510 --> 00:30:31.010
We all know that we love
our families desperately

00:30:31.010 --> 00:30:32.710
and that our families
are a little bit broken

00:30:32.710 --> 00:30:35.380
and have some baggage and some skeletons.

00:30:35.380 --> 00:30:36.740
We love what we do for a living

00:30:36.740 --> 00:30:38.910
and it's a headache and a drag.

00:30:38.910 --> 00:30:39.870
Everything's like that.

00:30:39.870 --> 00:30:43.363
We love ourselves, and we know
the ways in which we fail.

00:30:44.440 --> 00:30:47.510
Dune seems to model that nuanced, complex,

00:30:47.510 --> 00:30:51.100
multilayered thinking that
can entertain contradictions

00:30:51.100 --> 00:30:54.140
and complexities at every
level of the storytelling.

00:30:54.140 --> 00:30:55.320
And when I first read it,

00:30:55.320 --> 00:30:57.720
I think I had never
read a piece of fiction

00:30:57.720 --> 00:31:00.010
in which characters thought like that.

00:31:00.010 --> 00:31:00.903
And if there was anything

00:31:00.903 --> 00:31:04.360
I was hoping to preserve into
the film, it was that sense

00:31:04.360 --> 00:31:07.060
of many layered
comprehension of the world.

00:31:07.060 --> 00:31:11.100
- I would like go back
to this idea of pitfalls

00:31:11.100 --> 00:31:13.523
because I found it so
exciting to hear about.

00:31:14.490 --> 00:31:18.000
Denis, what were the greatest challenges

00:31:18.000 --> 00:31:20.290
in making this a script?

00:31:20.290 --> 00:31:23.440
You've already, all of you
have actually talked about

00:31:23.440 --> 00:31:25.890
the difficulty of compressing this

00:31:25.890 --> 00:31:28.370
and what you did in dividing it up.

00:31:28.370 --> 00:31:30.820
But are there other areas,

00:31:30.820 --> 00:31:32.090
are there areas where you think,

00:31:32.090 --> 00:31:34.470
wow, we really did this
exactly as I wanted,

00:31:34.470 --> 00:31:35.730
and some areas where you think,

00:31:35.730 --> 00:31:40.010
ugh, I wish I still had this, or I,

00:31:40.010 --> 00:31:41.960
you referenced some of Eric's work

00:31:41.960 --> 00:31:43.160
that you wish was still in it.

00:31:43.160 --> 00:31:46.243
But what was really, really hard here?

00:31:47.220 --> 00:31:49.550
- The thing is that, I
will state the obvious.

00:31:49.550 --> 00:31:54.383
The idea was that it was important
for me to honor the dream

00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:57.043
that when I read the book, to try,

00:31:58.703 --> 00:31:59.917
I had an appetite for that book,

00:31:59.917 --> 00:32:02.400
and I wanted the people
who had loved the book

00:32:02.400 --> 00:32:07.007
as much as I did will find
some respect of the world,

00:32:07.922 --> 00:32:10.114
of the poetry of the book.

00:32:10.114 --> 00:32:10.947
At the same time,

00:32:10.947 --> 00:32:12.370
I wanted my mother to
understand the movie.

00:32:12.370 --> 00:32:15.560
I wanted, to the people that don't,

00:32:15.560 --> 00:32:18.390
knew nothing about this
world will feel welcome,

00:32:18.390 --> 00:32:22.750
and so to balance information.

00:32:22.750 --> 00:32:26.483
There are so many cultures
and so many tribes in this,

00:32:27.630 --> 00:32:30.313
to find the right balance, to give enough,

00:32:32.790 --> 00:32:36.013
and not put too much
exposition at the beginning

00:32:39.370 --> 00:32:42.790
that would crush the, that the
audience would feel crushed

00:32:42.790 --> 00:32:44.660
under the amount of information.

00:32:44.660 --> 00:32:48.880
That was probably, for
me, the main challenge,

00:32:48.880 --> 00:32:50.047
I would say that at the
beginning of the book,

00:32:50.047 --> 00:32:52.710
the book started by saying something

00:32:52.710 --> 00:32:55.720
like beginnings are very delicate times.

00:32:55.720 --> 00:32:57.817
And I will say that definitely,

00:32:57.817 --> 00:33:00.907
how to open this movie
was the big challenge,

00:33:00.907 --> 00:33:02.360
how to approach it.

00:33:02.360 --> 00:33:05.280
At the beginning, we tried to
be more faithful to the book

00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:06.530
and realized quickly that,

00:33:06.530 --> 00:33:08.150
not quickly, not quickly.

00:33:08.150 --> 00:33:09.940
We realized at one point,
after a lot of work,

00:33:09.940 --> 00:33:13.730
that we had to deviate a
little bit from the book

00:33:13.730 --> 00:33:17.400
in order to make a soft,
a more gentle introduction

00:33:17.400 --> 00:33:20.463
to the world so people
will have enough knowledge

00:33:23.010 --> 00:33:25.633
to not feel lost at the beginning.

00:33:26.760 --> 00:33:30.130
It's a bit boring answer,
but that's the truth.

00:33:30.130 --> 00:33:32.040
- What about for you, Eric?

00:33:32.040 --> 00:33:35.240
What were the pitfalls
you saw going into it?

00:33:35.240 --> 00:33:37.420
- Well, I find the same pitfalls

00:33:37.420 --> 00:33:39.220
in a lot of science fiction.

00:33:39.220 --> 00:33:41.603
As I say, I'm not as steeped in it.

00:33:42.450 --> 00:33:44.673
Sometimes I find things hard to believe,

00:33:46.300 --> 00:33:48.650
and I found some of that in
this book, to be quite honest.

00:33:48.650 --> 00:33:51.403
It's just my probably
skeptical nature of things.

00:33:54.840 --> 00:33:56.632
- And I love you for that. (laughing)

00:33:56.632 --> 00:33:57.465
- Thank you.

00:33:57.465 --> 00:34:00.563
I mean, I like to poke
sticks in eyes a little bit.

00:34:01.940 --> 00:34:06.940
So I think it was not to emphasize cliches

00:34:08.500 --> 00:34:13.500
that sometimes arise and to
make it as literate as possible.

00:34:15.650 --> 00:34:20.650
I mean, you can take other
avenues to make the people real,

00:34:21.190 --> 00:34:22.060
like in any movie.

00:34:22.060 --> 00:34:24.870
So I mean, I think I approached
it the way I do any job

00:34:24.870 --> 00:34:27.500
that you have different
voices of different people

00:34:27.500 --> 00:34:30.320
that have different psychological aspects

00:34:30.320 --> 00:34:32.193
and come from different places.

00:34:33.531 --> 00:34:37.300
And it's really no different
than if you're writing a sci,

00:34:37.300 --> 00:34:39.520
I mean, I think I've said
this once to Denis before,

00:34:39.520 --> 00:34:42.490
that I was once talking to Francis Coppola

00:34:42.490 --> 00:34:44.110
about writing something and he said,

00:34:44.110 --> 00:34:45.367
a science fiction thing, and he said,

00:34:45.367 --> 00:34:48.067
"Well, as long as you're
not watching a chandelier,

00:34:48.067 --> 00:34:48.900
"it's fine."

00:34:48.900 --> 00:34:52.060
So you have the same rules
apply, in other words,

00:34:52.060 --> 00:34:53.530
to Shakespeare or anything else,

00:34:53.530 --> 00:34:55.460
that the characters have to resonate,

00:34:55.460 --> 00:34:59.650
have some emotional balance,
all those things, or imbalance.

00:34:59.650 --> 00:35:03.860
So I'd say those are the
same problems, and that,

00:35:03.860 --> 00:35:06.470
there is a weight of
the fact that it's Dune.

00:35:06.470 --> 00:35:09.638
In other words, I would say
I haven't adapted anything,

00:35:09.638 --> 00:35:13.340
that I can think of that consequence.

00:35:13.340 --> 00:35:15.080
It's like the famous thing

00:35:15.080 --> 00:35:17.220
is that bad books make good movies.

00:35:17.220 --> 00:35:18.630
And not that Dune's a bad book,

00:35:18.630 --> 00:35:20.940
but then I'm safer with Forrest Gump.

00:35:20.940 --> 00:35:21.773
You know what I'm saying?

00:35:21.773 --> 00:35:24.070
Or a bad play or something

00:35:24.070 --> 00:35:26.190
because you can go off
in flights are fancy

00:35:26.190 --> 00:35:28.120
and bend as you will.

00:35:28.120 --> 00:35:30.398
You couldn't do that here.

00:35:30.398 --> 00:35:35.230
I think there was
obligations, and I think that,

00:35:35.230 --> 00:35:37.130
thanks to Denis and Jon,
they were maintained.

00:35:37.130 --> 00:35:39.450
I'm not sure, if it
was left to my devices,

00:35:39.450 --> 00:35:42.200
it would be the same movie,
to be honest with you but--

00:35:42.200 --> 00:35:46.490
- And do you recall a
particular scene or element

00:35:46.490 --> 00:35:47.723
that gave you grief?

00:35:48.690 --> 00:35:50.310
- No, not really, not really.

00:35:50.310 --> 00:35:51.143
I think it was,

00:35:51.143 --> 00:35:54.410
no, I think everything had its challenges

00:35:54.410 --> 00:35:56.173
and also had its solutions.

00:35:57.040 --> 00:36:00.410
I did, I mean, I wish I had
had Jon at my right hand

00:36:00.410 --> 00:36:01.390
who knows the book so well.

00:36:01.390 --> 00:36:04.873
I did have a woman who was a PhD in Dune.

00:36:06.850 --> 00:36:11.220
So she told me what was the
gospel, according to her anyway,

00:36:11.220 --> 00:36:13.080
and I thought that was impressive.

00:36:13.080 --> 00:36:14.000
And she did know the thing.

00:36:14.000 --> 00:36:15.563
I think she was helpful to me.

00:36:17.020 --> 00:36:20.470
But I mean, I guess, I'm
being honest with you,

00:36:20.470 --> 00:36:22.200
I don't approach things very differently

00:36:22.200 --> 00:36:24.110
from anything I decide to do.

00:36:24.110 --> 00:36:25.800
And the bigger problem for me

00:36:25.800 --> 00:36:27.530
is the decision to decide to do it

00:36:28.500 --> 00:36:31.340
because I don't wanna really
fall on my face, you know.

00:36:31.340 --> 00:36:33.440
I'd rather that I left with a nice legacy.

00:36:33.440 --> 00:36:35.360
But anyway, thanks to these guys,

00:36:35.360 --> 00:36:37.370
it ended up being something special.

00:36:37.370 --> 00:36:40.290
- And Denis, was that for
you a difficult decision

00:36:40.290 --> 00:36:44.150
when you decided to split this into two?

00:36:44.150 --> 00:36:46.420
It sounds like it was
a necessary decision,

00:36:46.420 --> 00:36:50.540
but it also alters
structurally what you're doing.

00:36:50.540 --> 00:36:53.420
- It was very spontaneous,

00:36:53.420 --> 00:36:57.406
I think coming from an instinct
of survival. (laughing)

00:36:57.406 --> 00:36:58.590
- You said, "What am I gonna do?"

00:36:58.590 --> 00:37:01.573
- So it's like, I
remember once, I took the,

00:37:03.490 --> 00:37:05.972
I made the deal with the Jen Derry.

00:37:05.972 --> 00:37:08.300
And of course, first thing you
do is you go back to the book

00:37:08.300 --> 00:37:11.750
and spontaneously, I went back.

00:37:11.750 --> 00:37:14.300
The first thing I said, that
we have to make two movies,

00:37:14.300 --> 00:37:18.093
part one, part two, and
that was, and it was not,

00:37:19.492 --> 00:37:21.420
I didn't have to convince nobody.

00:37:21.420 --> 00:37:23.713
Everybody agreed spontaneously.

00:37:23.713 --> 00:37:24.960
(Vanessa laughs)

00:37:24.960 --> 00:37:29.960
And now, the challenge was
where to end the first part,

00:37:32.010 --> 00:37:34.060
and then that was like
a lot of exploration.

00:37:34.060 --> 00:37:36.090
There was different ideas on the table.

00:37:36.090 --> 00:37:37.950
- And how did you make that decision?

00:37:37.950 --> 00:37:41.730
What were some different
points you were considering?

00:37:41.730 --> 00:37:46.730
- Well, as Jon could be more
eloquent than me about that,

00:37:46.936 --> 00:37:50.350
but I would say that there is in the book

00:37:50.350 --> 00:37:52.350
a natural breaking point.

00:37:52.350 --> 00:37:55.243
There's a moment where
there's a time lapse,

00:37:55.243 --> 00:37:57.230
there's a time jump, sorry, between,

00:37:57.230 --> 00:37:59.420
and that would have been a natural way

00:37:59.420 --> 00:38:01.440
to end the first part.

00:38:01.440 --> 00:38:02.920
But the problem with that is that

00:38:02.920 --> 00:38:06.393
it meant that our main
characters, Paul and Jessica,

00:38:08.288 --> 00:38:11.740
had to be introduced to a
new culture and learn things

00:38:11.740 --> 00:38:16.170
and went through a process of
being accepted by the Fremen,

00:38:16.170 --> 00:38:19.090
and that felt like the
beginning of a story.

00:38:19.090 --> 00:38:22.437
It didn't feel like a last act of a movie.

00:38:22.437 --> 00:38:24.990
It felt like suddenly, we
were starting something,

00:38:24.990 --> 00:38:29.110
opening a new door, and it felt too heavy

00:38:30.545 --> 00:38:33.457
to put that at the end of the project.

00:38:33.457 --> 00:38:35.420
It really felt a disequilibrium.

00:38:35.420 --> 00:38:38.883
And we tried and there was
beauty in it, but it didn't,

00:38:40.830 --> 00:38:44.020
quickly, we realized that
it would be better to end

00:38:44.020 --> 00:38:47.850
as Paul and Jessica were
going toward this culture

00:38:47.850 --> 00:38:49.690
instead of embracing it.

00:38:49.690 --> 00:38:50.740
That's what I would say.

00:38:50.740 --> 00:38:52.470
Does it make sense what I'm saying?

00:38:52.470 --> 00:38:54.863
- Yes. (laughing)

00:38:56.290 --> 00:38:58.640
Hmm, yes.

00:38:58.640 --> 00:38:59.473
Yeah.

00:38:59.473 --> 00:39:04.340
And Jon, were you sort of
involved in that calibration?

00:39:04.340 --> 00:39:07.848
- Deeply, and we tried many things.

00:39:07.848 --> 00:39:08.681
- Yeah.

00:39:08.681 --> 00:39:10.670
- And we were trying to thread the needle

00:39:10.670 --> 00:39:12.530
in a bunch of ways.

00:39:12.530 --> 00:39:15.610
That time jump was the
first obvious target,

00:39:15.610 --> 00:39:18.700
because if you can go
away after the first film

00:39:18.700 --> 00:39:21.180
and then make a second
movie and you come back

00:39:21.180 --> 00:39:23.210
and it's two or three
years later in reality

00:39:23.210 --> 00:39:25.070
and it's also two or three
years later in the story,

00:39:25.070 --> 00:39:26.293
everything works out.

00:39:27.478 --> 00:39:29.100
You can have your actors
as tanned and bearded

00:39:29.100 --> 00:39:32.550
as you need them to be to be
themselves a few years older.

00:39:32.550 --> 00:39:37.550
So it did seem like a natural
target, but it thrust us

00:39:37.680 --> 00:39:40.460
toward that Lawrence
of Arabia running time

00:39:40.460 --> 00:39:43.403
which can limit the life
and audience of a film.

00:39:45.289 --> 00:39:48.463
And as Denis said, it was
a kind of big fourth act.

00:39:49.810 --> 00:39:51.970
What we did find, I think in part,

00:39:51.970 --> 00:39:54.140
we only saw the clarity of it

00:39:54.140 --> 00:39:58.650
having winnowed the
story of the first film

00:39:58.650 --> 00:40:00.750
down to an elegant shape.

00:40:00.750 --> 00:40:02.960
A lot of things fell by the wayside.

00:40:02.960 --> 00:40:06.490
The guiding logic again and again proved

00:40:06.490 --> 00:40:09.740
that we are following the
Atreides family, and above all,

00:40:09.740 --> 00:40:14.020
we are following Paul Atreides
through a metamorphosis.

00:40:14.020 --> 00:40:16.950
And everything that does not
serve the telling of the story

00:40:16.950 --> 00:40:20.570
of that metamorphosis became deemphasized,

00:40:20.570 --> 00:40:21.970
even things we dearly loved.

00:40:22.860 --> 00:40:27.510
And when you focus so
narrowly on that clan

00:40:27.510 --> 00:40:30.200
and that boy coming of age,

00:40:30.200 --> 00:40:33.690
then you see a three-act story, a tragedy

00:40:33.690 --> 00:40:35.690
of being drawn out of the
world of the familiar,

00:40:35.690 --> 00:40:40.690
of contending in a strange
new land with sinister forces,

00:40:40.970 --> 00:40:43.900
of breaking with the family, of the fall,

00:40:43.900 --> 00:40:45.250
and then the gleam of hope at the end

00:40:45.250 --> 00:40:48.940
when the dynasty survives
in him and in his mother.

00:40:48.940 --> 00:40:51.200
And that felt Shakespearean.

00:40:51.200 --> 00:40:53.230
It felt like it had a natural arc.

00:40:53.230 --> 00:40:57.250
And so it started to
feel more and more alien

00:40:57.250 --> 00:41:00.070
to then tell another
whole leg of the story

00:41:00.070 --> 00:41:03.920
and start new thing 'cause the
tragedy had defined itself.

00:41:03.920 --> 00:41:05.610
But we really found it

00:41:05.610 --> 00:41:09.230
by trying to make the story
fit into the first film

00:41:09.230 --> 00:41:11.800
and focusing with increasing intensity

00:41:11.800 --> 00:41:14.020
on the family and the hero,

00:41:14.020 --> 00:41:17.010
and that showed us where
the story needed to stop,

00:41:17.010 --> 00:41:19.450
in the very moment where
he's leaving one world

00:41:19.450 --> 00:41:20.720
and entering another.

00:41:22.040 --> 00:41:25.060
- Yeah, I could see that
being very challenging.

00:41:26.858 --> 00:41:28.360
- Well, I think it was also complicated

00:41:28.360 --> 00:41:31.260
because there was a question of whether

00:41:31.260 --> 00:41:33.756
he should shoot two movies at once.

00:41:33.756 --> 00:41:34.589
- Mm-hmm.

00:41:34.589 --> 00:41:35.422
- [Vanessa] Right.

00:41:35.422 --> 00:41:36.490
- And that was just an interesting,

00:41:36.490 --> 00:41:38.500
in one way, you can save a lot of money.

00:41:38.500 --> 00:41:40.920
Another way, it can be a disaster,

00:41:40.920 --> 00:41:43.530
so there's some practical considerations.

00:41:43.530 --> 00:41:46.000
But when we were dealing
with the storytelling,

00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:48.760
it had to do with, well,
where are we gonna end this

00:41:48.760 --> 00:41:51.010
even if we're blending into movie two

00:41:51.010 --> 00:41:53.230
that you're gonna release a year from now,

00:41:53.230 --> 00:41:56.700
so it was really an interesting situation

00:41:56.700 --> 00:41:58.503
that resolved itself, fortunately.

00:42:00.910 --> 00:42:02.210
- I am going to take a moment

00:42:02.210 --> 00:42:04.420
to ask about a scene that I really loved

00:42:04.420 --> 00:42:05.950
and just see if anyone can remember

00:42:05.950 --> 00:42:07.393
the origins of this scene.

00:42:08.310 --> 00:42:11.130
I really enjoyed the scene where Duke Leto

00:42:11.130 --> 00:42:13.573
has to rescue the crew from the harvester.

00:42:14.980 --> 00:42:18.040
Was that a scene in the book?

00:42:18.040 --> 00:42:19.340
I don't, yes.

00:42:19.340 --> 00:42:21.610
- Yeah, it's a very good interesting scene

00:42:21.610 --> 00:42:25.170
where the three of us had
an impact on that scene.

00:42:25.170 --> 00:42:29.980
It's a famous scene in
the book, but in the book,

00:42:29.980 --> 00:42:31.790
let's say that Paul is less involved.

00:42:31.790 --> 00:42:33.080
He's more in the backseat.

00:42:33.080 --> 00:42:35.923
He's more like a spectator, yeah.

00:42:39.873 --> 00:42:40.800
And Eric brought the idea

00:42:40.800 --> 00:42:43.340
of Paul getting out of the ornithopter,

00:42:43.340 --> 00:42:46.560
bringing Paul involved,
involving Paul with the rescue,

00:42:46.560 --> 00:42:47.880
being on the ground.

00:42:47.880 --> 00:42:52.690
The idea was that it will
be his first encounter

00:42:52.690 --> 00:42:53.980
with the deep desert,

00:42:53.980 --> 00:42:56.110
that it will have a resonance inside him,

00:42:56.110 --> 00:42:59.150
that he will strangely feel like at home

00:42:59.150 --> 00:43:00.670
in this foreign landscape,

00:43:00.670 --> 00:43:04.470
and that moment, reorient
the scene in a different way.

00:43:04.470 --> 00:43:06.810
It was more than just a rescue.

00:43:06.810 --> 00:43:08.940
It was like an encounter,

00:43:08.940 --> 00:43:10.760
a spiritual encounter with the desert

00:43:10.760 --> 00:43:12.970
and with that creature that will come.

00:43:12.970 --> 00:43:15.910
And then from Eric ideas,

00:43:15.910 --> 00:43:19.283
Jon came and slightly shifted where,

00:43:20.920 --> 00:43:23.670
brought this idea of Paul
having hallucinations.

00:43:23.670 --> 00:43:25.443
I think that came from Jon.

00:43:26.410 --> 00:43:28.623
Sometimes, the truth is,

00:43:29.665 --> 00:43:32.220
and it's a thing I love about filmmaking,

00:43:32.220 --> 00:43:35.720
is collective creativity,
meaning that sometimes--

00:43:35.720 --> 00:43:37.047
- Remember--

00:43:37.047 --> 00:43:38.590
- I don't remember who
got the idea exactly.

00:43:38.590 --> 00:43:40.003
So gentlemen, if I'm,

00:43:41.570 --> 00:43:44.210
because there's so much decisions.

00:43:44.210 --> 00:43:45.690
- It doesn't matter.

00:43:45.690 --> 00:43:48.130
- But at the end, it's
the movie, it's teamwork.

00:43:48.130 --> 00:43:50.210
But I will say that
it's a beautiful scene,

00:43:50.210 --> 00:43:52.500
a scene that is a good
example of collaborative

00:43:52.500 --> 00:43:54.690
that evolve in a beautiful way

00:43:54.690 --> 00:43:57.950
to tie in the roots of the ideas

00:43:57.950 --> 00:44:01.290
and then evolve through
different ends on the keyboard.

00:44:01.290 --> 00:44:03.550
And I'm very, I love that scene.

00:44:03.550 --> 00:44:05.052
- Yeah, that is--

00:44:05.052 --> 00:44:05.885
- [Eric] Me too.

00:44:05.885 --> 00:44:07.240
- It's a very striking scene from the book

00:44:07.240 --> 00:44:09.100
because Dune is a novel that leaves

00:44:09.100 --> 00:44:13.253
a lot of its biggest
moments of action off stage.

00:44:14.240 --> 00:44:17.750
And you get reports of this
great big piece of action

00:44:17.750 --> 00:44:18.583
that just happened.

00:44:18.583 --> 00:44:21.323
You have characters discussing
the aftermath and reacting.

00:44:22.240 --> 00:44:24.450
But he just wasn't very
interested, for the most part,

00:44:24.450 --> 00:44:27.420
in writing a lot of big action
set pieces in his books,

00:44:27.420 --> 00:44:29.550
and so they tended to be in the background

00:44:29.550 --> 00:44:33.670
as like map paintings, and you
get actors in the foreground

00:44:33.670 --> 00:44:35.310
talking about what it all means.

00:44:35.310 --> 00:44:36.410
- Like theater.

00:44:36.410 --> 00:44:37.760
- Yeah, exactly.

00:44:37.760 --> 00:44:40.950
This is a scene in the book
that is operatically told

00:44:40.950 --> 00:44:45.070
as an action set piece,
really one of the only ones.

00:44:45.070 --> 00:44:46.870
And I think it's one of the scenes

00:44:46.870 --> 00:44:49.020
that everyone comes away
from the book remembering.

00:44:49.020 --> 00:44:51.750
There are a few things that
people who've read the book once

00:44:51.750 --> 00:44:54.110
a long time ago will tell you about,

00:44:54.110 --> 00:44:56.010
and they'll tell you about the Gom Jabbar,

00:44:56.010 --> 00:44:59.040
and they'll tell sandworm
eating the sand crawler,

00:44:59.040 --> 00:45:01.070
and they'll remember that moment.

00:45:01.070 --> 00:45:04.240
So it was an exciting scene
to focus on, but there too,

00:45:04.240 --> 00:45:07.270
like every scene in the novel,

00:45:07.270 --> 00:45:09.770
it has too many layers and
too many conversations in it

00:45:09.770 --> 00:45:11.100
to fit into the film.

00:45:11.100 --> 00:45:14.640
And so there, again, it was a
process of iteratively finding

00:45:14.640 --> 00:45:16.810
the essence of the sequence.

00:45:16.810 --> 00:45:20.330
- And was this quite an
iterative process in general?

00:45:20.330 --> 00:45:25.200
Would you say, for all of you,
compared to other projects,

00:45:25.200 --> 00:45:27.750
films you've worked on,
was this quite iterative?

00:45:27.750 --> 00:45:30.610
Were there many, many, many,
many, many, many drafts,

00:45:30.610 --> 00:45:31.810
or just sort of,

00:45:31.810 --> 00:45:33.350
yes, okay, a yes.

00:45:33.350 --> 00:45:36.530
- Yeah, I mean, I, Eric
and Denis can talk,

00:45:36.530 --> 00:45:38.570
Eric can talk about his process with Denis

00:45:38.570 --> 00:45:42.150
'cause that preceded me, but for my part,

00:45:42.150 --> 00:45:46.630
the shape of it was that I
came in and took the book apart

00:45:46.630 --> 00:45:49.730
and did a big adaptive work of my own,

00:45:49.730 --> 00:45:52.700
which then amalgamated
with Eric and Denis' work

00:45:52.700 --> 00:45:55.120
and pulled all of their finest
creative flights into it

00:45:55.120 --> 00:45:57.230
and sort of mushed it
together in one thing.

00:45:57.230 --> 00:45:59.540
And then Denis and I turned that over

00:45:59.540 --> 00:46:02.340
again and again and
again four or five times

00:46:02.340 --> 00:46:05.280
in these very intensely
collaborative writing sessions

00:46:05.280 --> 00:46:07.370
where we'd lock ourselves
in a hotel suite for a week.

00:46:07.370 --> 00:46:09.810
And he would come and go,
and when he left, I'd write.

00:46:09.810 --> 00:46:10.760
He'd come back the next day

00:46:10.760 --> 00:46:14.440
and we'd talk from nine
until two in the afternoon.

00:46:14.440 --> 00:46:15.480
I would write until midnight

00:46:15.480 --> 00:46:17.430
and send him my pages and go to sleep.

00:46:17.430 --> 00:46:19.170
And he'd come in in the
morning having read them,

00:46:19.170 --> 00:46:22.250
and we'd talk until two or
three, and we'd do it again

00:46:22.250 --> 00:46:23.350
until by the end of the week,

00:46:23.350 --> 00:46:24.950
he would leave at two or
three in the afternoon.

00:46:24.950 --> 00:46:27.370
I would instantly fall asleep
and sleep until midnight

00:46:27.370 --> 00:46:29.410
and wake up and then write
until six in the morning,

00:46:29.410 --> 00:46:31.430
send him my pages.

00:46:31.430 --> 00:46:34.287
- Now I'm curious by this
process, and I would love to know,

00:46:34.287 --> 00:46:37.193
Denis, if you have pursued
this process in the past.

00:46:38.790 --> 00:46:39.623
- Not, not, no.

00:46:39.623 --> 00:46:41.840
It was the first time
that I was working with,

00:46:42.930 --> 00:46:43.763
it's not true.

00:46:43.763 --> 00:46:45.450
I had wrote myself a screenplay

00:46:45.450 --> 00:46:47.463
with the help of another writer.

00:46:48.510 --> 00:46:50.112
But after that,

00:46:50.112 --> 00:46:53.460
I came onboard a project that
had been written already,

00:46:53.460 --> 00:46:54.820
so it was a different approach.

00:46:54.820 --> 00:46:58.010
It was like to try to bring a screenplay

00:46:58.010 --> 00:47:02.770
closer to my sensibility by
rewriting with the writer,

00:47:02.770 --> 00:47:06.143
story boarding, rewriting again, but not,

00:47:07.160 --> 00:47:10.555
starting from scratch like
that with Eric and Jon, it was,

00:47:10.555 --> 00:47:11.420
it had been a long time,

00:47:11.420 --> 00:47:14.763
and I was missing that
part of the process.

00:47:16.304 --> 00:47:19.080
Writing is so, it's so
difficult, but it's so beautiful.

00:47:19.080 --> 00:47:21.773
I mean, I'm grateful that
I had a chance to work.

00:47:23.050 --> 00:47:25.810
Even on the side, even if
I was just on the side,

00:47:25.810 --> 00:47:29.560
I had great fun watching
Eric and Jon working.

00:47:30.473 --> 00:47:32.475
For me, it was fascinating.

00:47:32.475 --> 00:47:33.308
- Yeah.

00:47:33.308 --> 00:47:35.020
- At the very end of the process,

00:47:35.020 --> 00:47:36.960
when there were particular
points of the movie

00:47:36.960 --> 00:47:41.900
that were focuses of Denis'
creative concentration,

00:47:41.900 --> 00:47:43.573
then there was a lot of overleaving

00:47:43.573 --> 00:47:46.393
where Denis was like the
conductor in orchestra.

00:47:46.393 --> 00:47:47.370
And there were things Eric came in touch

00:47:47.370 --> 00:47:48.710
and I came in touch.

00:47:48.710 --> 00:47:50.660
We were writing to a line by line level

00:47:50.660 --> 00:47:54.510
and just sanding and sanding
until the thing took--

00:47:54.510 --> 00:47:57.450
- Yeah, because at one point,
what happened, like any movie,

00:47:57.450 --> 00:48:00.623
was the reality team of
the production, a budget,

00:48:02.270 --> 00:48:04.930
restrictions, which is part of the,

00:48:04.930 --> 00:48:09.056
and then that's, Jon
came, Eric came, and we,

00:48:09.056 --> 00:48:10.430
so it was like a sculpture at that moment.

00:48:10.430 --> 00:48:12.923
Everybody was chipping to make sure

00:48:12.923 --> 00:48:15.830
that it will finally have the most

00:48:15.830 --> 00:48:18.680
let's say beautiful shape
in the circumstances.

00:48:18.680 --> 00:48:22.120
- Now we all know the saying
too many cooks spoil the,

00:48:22.120 --> 00:48:23.885
whatever it is it spoils.
- Broth.

00:48:23.885 --> 00:48:25.306
- [Vanessa] Apparently, I
don't know the saying, but--

00:48:25.306 --> 00:48:26.139
- Broth.

00:48:26.139 --> 00:48:29.240
- It sounds like the
opposite in this case.

00:48:29.240 --> 00:48:31.340
To what do you attribute that?

00:48:31.340 --> 00:48:32.835
Is there anything to,

00:48:32.835 --> 00:48:36.950
do you know why is it
simply that each of you,

00:48:36.950 --> 00:48:40.770
you had complimentary skills
or perhaps personalities?

00:48:40.770 --> 00:48:42.650
- There was the book, as Eric said,

00:48:42.650 --> 00:48:44.280
mentioned at the beginning.

00:48:44.280 --> 00:48:45.337
We had obligation toward the book,

00:48:45.337 --> 00:48:47.070
and it was like it's an object

00:48:47.070 --> 00:48:49.023
that as such a strong persona.

00:48:50.230 --> 00:48:51.567
Either you like or you
don't like the book,

00:48:51.567 --> 00:48:54.070
it still has a strong personality,

00:48:54.070 --> 00:48:57.080
and I think that it helped us to shape.

00:48:57.080 --> 00:48:59.040
I think that's my take on it.

00:48:59.040 --> 00:49:02.816
- But I do think, just since
we're talking to writers here,

00:49:02.816 --> 00:49:05.410
I think this is a unique situation.

00:49:05.410 --> 00:49:08.690
I've been through some,
as I said, many of these

00:49:08.690 --> 00:49:11.620
where you have conflicting
kind of writing.

00:49:11.620 --> 00:49:13.770
One person writes
different than the other,

00:49:15.472 --> 00:49:17.040
and it can get very kind of hairy.

00:49:17.040 --> 00:49:19.660
And rewriting is very difficult

00:49:19.660 --> 00:49:22.030
because you're trying to maintain a tone

00:49:22.030 --> 00:49:26.070
and not have a separate
voice and change everything.

00:49:26.070 --> 00:49:26.903
I think Denis is right.

00:49:26.903 --> 00:49:27.940
I mean, I think the book

00:49:27.940 --> 00:49:29.950
is such a grounded centerpiece for this.

00:49:29.950 --> 00:49:33.130
And then everybody had some great respect

00:49:33.130 --> 00:49:35.620
for what it was had to say,

00:49:35.620 --> 00:49:37.070
what we would like to say with it.

00:49:37.070 --> 00:49:41.150
And Denis was a wonderful
maestro, a wonderful master

00:49:41.150 --> 00:49:44.060
of keeping all the boats moving in line.

00:49:44.060 --> 00:49:46.000
So it was a unique,

00:49:46.000 --> 00:49:46.833
I'm telling you.

00:49:46.833 --> 00:49:50.340
I've had a lot of 'em which
didn't work out so good.

00:49:50.340 --> 00:49:51.963
- But isn't it interesting too,

00:49:51.963 --> 00:49:54.090
because I don't think
one can always correlate

00:49:54.090 --> 00:49:58.483
those wonderful, magical work
relationships with good films.

00:49:59.630 --> 00:50:00.584
I mean, I think--
- Actually, that's true.

00:50:00.584 --> 00:50:02.211
- No, I'm agreeing, I'm agreeing
because I think that's--

00:50:02.211 --> 00:50:04.320
- So it's shocking that
that is what happened.

00:50:04.320 --> 00:50:06.230
- Well, varying voices don't,

00:50:06.230 --> 00:50:08.313
sometimes don't make one point of view.

00:50:09.520 --> 00:50:11.630
I think people can take
advantage of that too.

00:50:11.630 --> 00:50:13.611
That's a whole different conversation.

00:50:13.611 --> 00:50:14.570
- Mm-hmm, it's true, yeah.

00:50:14.570 --> 00:50:15.763
It's true what you're saying, Eric.

00:50:15.763 --> 00:50:18.590
- I think I may have gone over my time,

00:50:18.590 --> 00:50:22.010
so I think I better announce the Q&amp;A

00:50:22.010 --> 00:50:24.490
with the Academy Gold Alumni

00:50:24.490 --> 00:50:27.373
and go to our first
questioner, Haley Bartel.

00:50:30.090 --> 00:50:30.923
- Hi, everybody.

00:50:30.923 --> 00:50:32.360
Thank you so much for being here.

00:50:32.360 --> 00:50:34.720
This has been amazing.

00:50:34.720 --> 00:50:39.060
My brain is exploding, and huge
congratulations on the film.

00:50:39.060 --> 00:50:42.370
So my question is for everyone here,

00:50:42.370 --> 00:50:45.790
and it's kind of a general
world building question.

00:50:45.790 --> 00:50:49.300
So when you were conceiving
the worlds of your films,

00:50:49.300 --> 00:50:54.300
are you intentionally
thinking of and constructing

00:50:54.390 --> 00:50:59.030
internal elements that reflect
sort of the internal elements

00:50:59.030 --> 00:51:00.850
of your characters' arc and theme?

00:51:00.850 --> 00:51:04.263
Or do the worlds sort of
spring fully formed as Athena?

00:51:05.750 --> 00:51:06.830
- What is it?

00:51:06.830 --> 00:51:10.340
Your question is about the use
of landscape or architecture

00:51:10.340 --> 00:51:13.813
to express the internal
journey of the characters,

00:51:13.813 --> 00:51:15.710
is that what you're saying?

00:51:15.710 --> 00:51:18.260
- Yeah, just the sort of integral elements

00:51:18.260 --> 00:51:20.130
that exist in the world of the film

00:51:20.130 --> 00:51:22.133
as you're creating that world.

00:51:23.795 --> 00:51:24.628
- I apologize.

00:51:24.628 --> 00:51:25.930
Maybe I'm misunderstanding your question,

00:51:25.930 --> 00:51:28.680
but definitely, there was like,

00:51:28.680 --> 00:51:32.310
landscape was a very important component

00:51:32.310 --> 00:51:36.480
of the storytelling here where the way,

00:51:36.480 --> 00:51:40.210
if you look at the way the
movie evolves, the color palette

00:51:40.210 --> 00:51:43.740
and the way we are embracing the landscape

00:51:43.740 --> 00:51:48.740
at different moment was
used as a way to externalize

00:51:51.830 --> 00:51:54.880
the condition in which
the main character, Paul,

00:51:54.880 --> 00:51:57.830
because the idea was that we
will focus as much as possible

00:51:59.725 --> 00:52:01.830
the story on Paul, his point of view.

00:52:01.830 --> 00:52:03.930
In a perfect world, I would
have made just the movie

00:52:03.930 --> 00:52:05.650
about Paul and his mother,

00:52:05.650 --> 00:52:07.840
but we had to go outside from time to time

00:52:07.840 --> 00:52:09.987
in order to understand the story.

00:52:09.987 --> 00:52:14.987
And definitely, architecture
and landscape were used

00:52:17.120 --> 00:52:19.110
in a cinematic way, I think, to express.

00:52:19.110 --> 00:52:20.810
And it's the beauty of
doing science fiction

00:52:20.810 --> 00:52:22.150
because you can create everything,

00:52:22.150 --> 00:52:23.190
so you have like the full,

00:52:23.190 --> 00:52:24.070
you are like a god.

00:52:24.070 --> 00:52:27.610
You can create all the elements
you need in order to express

00:52:30.230 --> 00:52:33.773
some psychological component of the,

00:52:35.147 --> 00:52:36.000
I don't know if what I'm saying

00:52:36.000 --> 00:52:39.310
makes sense in English
right now. (laughing)

00:52:39.310 --> 00:52:40.143
Yeah.

00:52:40.143 --> 00:52:41.190
- [Eric] It does.

00:52:41.190 --> 00:52:43.960
- That's the thing I love
about sci-fi is that you,

00:52:43.960 --> 00:52:45.470
it's like a living sculpture

00:52:45.470 --> 00:52:48.670
where you are actually
having an impact on the,

00:52:48.670 --> 00:52:51.260
but it will be the same
with any movies, in fact.

00:52:52.800 --> 00:52:56.973
It's part of the beauty
of finding resonance

00:52:57.850 --> 00:53:00.290
into the elements or into the landscape

00:53:00.290 --> 00:53:02.210
in order to help the audience

00:53:03.360 --> 00:53:08.360
to understand characters in their journey.

00:53:08.650 --> 00:53:09.860
- All right.

00:53:09.860 --> 00:53:12.990
Our next question is from Desmond Jackson.

00:53:12.990 --> 00:53:16.320
- Now, if I may add, the
story we're following

00:53:16.320 --> 00:53:17.970
is the story of a boy.

00:53:17.970 --> 00:53:20.130
The deeper he goes into the desert,

00:53:20.130 --> 00:53:21.650
the more he learn about himself,

00:53:21.650 --> 00:53:24.100
the more he has an introspective journey.

00:53:24.100 --> 00:53:25.640
It's a very introspective movie,

00:53:25.640 --> 00:53:29.020
so it's like that landscape,
that tremendous impact.

00:53:29.020 --> 00:53:31.190
That's why the movie was
shot with massive wides

00:53:31.190 --> 00:53:32.750
and very intimate shots.

00:53:32.750 --> 00:53:34.100
There was no in between a lot.

00:53:34.100 --> 00:53:38.020
It's like, it's really
embracing that point of view

00:53:38.020 --> 00:53:42.820
of a boy discovering a
new world, a new culture

00:53:42.820 --> 00:53:44.170
and embracing that culture.

00:53:46.420 --> 00:53:47.470
- All right, Desmond?

00:53:50.040 --> 00:53:50.873
- Hi, guys.

00:53:50.873 --> 00:53:51.706
Thank you so much.

00:53:51.706 --> 00:53:53.290
Y'all have literally been dropping gems

00:53:53.290 --> 00:53:55.267
throughout this whole session,

00:53:55.267 --> 00:53:57.820
so thank you so much, and fantastic film.

00:53:57.820 --> 00:53:59.580
My question is also similar to Haley's

00:53:59.580 --> 00:54:00.800
in terms of world building.

00:54:00.800 --> 00:54:04.340
I wanted to know if you guys
had any specific suggestions

00:54:04.340 --> 00:54:06.770
in terms of introducing worlds

00:54:06.770 --> 00:54:09.210
and the rules and class systems and races

00:54:09.210 --> 00:54:11.520
and different planet
structures and everything

00:54:11.520 --> 00:54:14.640
to like an unfamiliar audience
without being too expository

00:54:14.640 --> 00:54:16.140
with the character's dialogue.

00:54:17.210 --> 00:54:21.893
- That's, if I were gonna talk
about the pitfalls of Dune,

00:54:22.780 --> 00:54:24.783
I would talk about the rules.

00:54:27.570 --> 00:54:30.000
I grew up reading a ton of
fantasy and science fiction

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:33.410
when I was just a kid, and
then I got tired of it.

00:54:33.410 --> 00:54:34.243
I put it away.

00:54:34.243 --> 00:54:35.940
And when I was in high school and college,

00:54:35.940 --> 00:54:37.200
I was reading literary fiction

00:54:37.200 --> 00:54:39.360
and I was going to be a novelist someday.

00:54:39.360 --> 00:54:41.290
And I didn't really come
back to science fiction

00:54:41.290 --> 00:54:42.570
until I started screenwriting,

00:54:42.570 --> 00:54:43.970
where there was an appetite for it,

00:54:43.970 --> 00:54:48.450
and I had a foundation, and I
ended up working in this file,

00:54:48.450 --> 00:54:51.260
but a lot of my heart
was in the philosophical

00:54:51.260 --> 00:54:53.910
and very personal investigations
of literary fiction.

00:54:55.030 --> 00:54:57.970
There was a way to write Dune
where you kind of went deep

00:54:57.970 --> 00:55:00.700
and Dungeons and Dragonsy
about all the rules

00:55:00.700 --> 00:55:01.860
and the orders and the people

00:55:01.860 --> 00:55:05.350
and just really got into the
weeds of the world building.

00:55:05.350 --> 00:55:08.300
And there can be fun in that,

00:55:08.300 --> 00:55:10.880
but there's very little poetry in it.

00:55:10.880 --> 00:55:13.300
And above all, it gobbles oxygen.

00:55:13.300 --> 00:55:15.770
In any movie, there's not much space.

00:55:15.770 --> 00:55:16.700
Movies are small.

00:55:16.700 --> 00:55:17.960
They are novellas.

00:55:17.960 --> 00:55:19.850
And so everything you put in

00:55:19.850 --> 00:55:22.100
goes into the expense of something else.

00:55:22.100 --> 00:55:26.070
And the more you are putting
in the Tin Makers Guild,

00:55:26.070 --> 00:55:30.250
and the dragons of Cora,
and the nebulons of Troy,

00:55:30.250 --> 00:55:32.170
whatever you've invented for your world,

00:55:32.170 --> 00:55:34.220
the more you talk about that,
the less your characters

00:55:34.220 --> 00:55:36.550
are living and breathing
in front of the audience.

00:55:36.550 --> 00:55:40.450
And so the beautiful guiding
light in this production

00:55:40.450 --> 00:55:43.770
was Denis and his desire to tell a story

00:55:43.770 --> 00:55:46.090
through the pure language of cinema.

00:55:46.090 --> 00:55:47.380
And he's saying that

00:55:47.380 --> 00:55:50.260
about one of the talkiest
novels in science fiction,

00:55:50.260 --> 00:55:52.457
and he says, "My goal, my ideal here

00:55:52.457 --> 00:55:54.217
"would be a nearly silent film."

00:55:55.607 --> 00:55:57.728
- With that, we failed. (laughing)

00:55:57.728 --> 00:55:58.607
- (laughing) I know.

00:55:58.607 --> 00:56:01.030
- But as a compass needle,

00:56:01.030 --> 00:56:05.410
that guided us in the
exposition of everything.

00:56:05.410 --> 00:56:08.550
So to answer your question
very programmatically,

00:56:08.550 --> 00:56:11.520
if you wanna build a fantasy
world with intricate rules,

00:56:11.520 --> 00:56:14.910
do write them down, get Dungeons
and Dragonsy about them.

00:56:14.910 --> 00:56:17.290
Have your taxonomies, your histories,

00:56:17.290 --> 00:56:19.400
and then don't ever talk about it.

00:56:19.400 --> 00:56:20.740
You don't need to.

00:56:20.740 --> 00:56:23.500
It will color the work
because you know it.

00:56:23.500 --> 00:56:25.490
It will change the way
your characters speak

00:56:25.490 --> 00:56:27.130
because you know where they've come from,

00:56:27.130 --> 00:56:28.550
you know how they live.

00:56:28.550 --> 00:56:29.680
It will be implied.

00:56:29.680 --> 00:56:30.690
It will be poetry.

00:56:30.690 --> 00:56:32.020
It will be in the background,

00:56:32.020 --> 00:56:33.570
and that's where it should live.

00:56:33.570 --> 00:56:34.530
What you want to be doing

00:56:34.530 --> 00:56:37.313
is telling human stories
in the foreground.

00:56:38.160 --> 00:56:40.380
And so you wanna be as
economical as possible

00:56:40.380 --> 00:56:43.220
about actually being explicit

00:56:43.220 --> 00:56:44.883
about any of that world building.

00:56:46.910 --> 00:56:47.890
- Thank you.

00:56:47.890 --> 00:56:50.123
Our next question is from Marlon Desilva.

00:56:51.200 --> 00:56:52.150
- Thank you so much, Vanessa,

00:56:52.150 --> 00:56:54.470
and thank you all for this
great insightful panel.

00:56:54.470 --> 00:56:55.810
I've learned so much.

00:56:55.810 --> 00:56:57.920
A question I had thinking
about the movie was,

00:56:57.920 --> 00:57:00.880
did you all have any visual
references to refer to

00:57:00.880 --> 00:57:01.920
while writing the screenplay?

00:57:01.920 --> 00:57:05.113
And how were they helpful when
bringing those words to life?

00:57:06.250 --> 00:57:07.750
- If I may, I would just say

00:57:07.750 --> 00:57:10.450
that something that was
fascinating for me is that

00:57:10.450 --> 00:57:14.300
when we started the work,
Eric started the work,

00:57:14.300 --> 00:57:16.590
and I was, at the time, doing Blade Runner

00:57:16.590 --> 00:57:20.170
and I was like overwhelmed
finishing the movie on time.

00:57:20.170 --> 00:57:22.460
And I remember that Eric had done,

00:57:22.460 --> 00:57:25.580
and still today, those images haunted me

00:57:25.580 --> 00:57:27.040
as I shot the movie.

00:57:27.040 --> 00:57:30.660
He had done a beautiful
research, historical research

00:57:30.660 --> 00:57:32.670
that involved science fiction images,

00:57:32.670 --> 00:57:36.570
but also historical based
on medieval research.

00:57:36.570 --> 00:57:40.697
It really, all those images helped me to,

00:57:40.697 --> 00:57:44.010
I was, at the time,
starting to shape the world.

00:57:44.010 --> 00:57:45.350
I had images in myself,

00:57:45.350 --> 00:57:49.040
but I remember the influence
of Eric research on his side

00:57:49.040 --> 00:57:52.570
that I thought was really
poetic and insightful.

00:57:52.570 --> 00:57:56.330
And then when Jon came on board,
at the time, it was later,

00:57:56.330 --> 00:58:01.330
so I had the time to start
concept art and had the time to,

00:58:03.520 --> 00:58:08.247
so when, I remember sometimes
Jon was writing and saying,

00:58:08.247 --> 00:58:10.680
"By the way, what does it look like?"

00:58:10.680 --> 00:58:11.950
And then I was showing him an image,

00:58:11.950 --> 00:58:15.940
and then he was like, "Whoa."

00:58:15.940 --> 00:58:19.180
It was interesting that
I was drawing the movie

00:58:19.180 --> 00:58:23.670
as it was written, and then
sometimes, I was sharing.

00:58:23.670 --> 00:58:25.620
Both, it was a dance between that.

00:58:25.620 --> 00:58:27.170
So that's what I wanted to say.

00:58:28.960 --> 00:58:29.793
- Thank you.

00:58:30.870 --> 00:58:33.913
And our last question
is from Magna Nagaraji.

00:58:35.940 --> 00:58:40.390
- Hi, you got my name
perfectly right. (laughing)

00:58:40.390 --> 00:58:42.520
So I really loved the movie,

00:58:42.520 --> 00:58:46.180
and thank you so much for
taking the time to speak to us.

00:58:46.180 --> 00:58:48.663
Obviously, the soundtrack
is very haunting,

00:58:49.600 --> 00:58:50.520
but here's my question.

00:58:50.520 --> 00:58:54.310
So Chani's character doesn't
have like a speaking part

00:58:54.310 --> 00:58:55.360
until like the last,

00:58:55.360 --> 00:58:57.650
like about 30 minutes of the film, right,

00:58:57.650 --> 00:59:01.970
but she has such a huge
presence throughout the movie.

00:59:01.970 --> 00:59:06.370
And I wanted to know if that's
something that you guys wrote

00:59:06.370 --> 00:59:09.774
or is that something
that was like directed?

00:59:09.774 --> 00:59:10.607
- Hmm.

00:59:12.824 --> 00:59:13.865
- It was written.

00:59:13.865 --> 00:59:15.527
- [Eric] I remember it was written.

00:59:15.527 --> 00:59:16.360
- Yes, yes, yes, it was written.

00:59:16.360 --> 00:59:17.723
- I think it's written too.

00:59:21.118 --> 00:59:21.968
- It was written.

00:59:22.809 --> 00:59:26.060
The idea here was that Paul is
obsessed with those visions.

00:59:26.060 --> 00:59:28.830
And then finally, when
he finally meets her,

00:59:28.830 --> 00:59:33.240
then there's a bit of an
exchange, a contact that is made.

00:59:33.240 --> 00:59:36.070
But of course, all this is promises,

00:59:36.070 --> 00:59:38.110
promise for the second movie.

00:59:38.110 --> 00:59:43.110
It's like, it's a bet that
we did with having at the end

00:59:44.040 --> 00:59:47.180
just to, we introduced characters that,

00:59:47.180 --> 00:59:50.770
and I was praying that they
would be a part two (laughing)

00:59:50.770 --> 00:59:53.251
because otherwise, it would be weird.

00:59:53.251 --> 00:59:54.300
But yeah, so.

00:59:54.300 --> 00:59:57.500
I don't know if you want to
add something, Jon or Eric.

00:59:57.500 --> 01:00:00.020
- Well, I think that intention was there,

01:00:00.020 --> 01:00:01.230
but I also think that

01:00:04.101 --> 01:00:07.790
the power of Chani's presence in the film

01:00:08.880 --> 01:00:12.220
is it flows directly from cinema.

01:00:12.220 --> 01:00:17.130
It's from the very potent
performance of Zendaya herself

01:00:17.130 --> 01:00:19.790
and the way Denis frames
her again and again

01:00:19.790 --> 01:00:21.523
in the language of dreams.

01:00:22.380 --> 01:00:26.320
She is the character who
most inter-penetrates

01:00:26.320 --> 01:00:29.180
the real world that Paul
is adventuring through

01:00:29.180 --> 01:00:31.850
and the prophetic world
that he's experiencing

01:00:31.850 --> 01:00:35.360
through his unreliable
glimpses of the future.

01:00:35.360 --> 01:00:38.470
And so because she lives
in both those worlds,

01:00:38.470 --> 01:00:40.160
she acquires a higher significance.

01:00:40.160 --> 01:00:42.240
And of course, she greets
us at the top of the movie.

01:00:42.240 --> 01:00:45.240
It's her voice who frames
the historical conflict

01:00:45.240 --> 01:00:47.530
that will play out throughout the film.

01:00:47.530 --> 01:00:49.963
- One thing I will say is that,

01:00:53.000 --> 01:00:56.730
I agree, of course, with
what Eric and Jon said,

01:00:56.730 --> 01:01:01.453
but I was, as a director, I
was mesmerized by Zendaya,

01:01:02.358 --> 01:01:04.733
by her presence, by her
power, the magnetism.

01:01:07.413 --> 01:01:08.870
And I shot more.

01:01:08.870 --> 01:01:09.760
I had more.

01:01:09.760 --> 01:01:14.760
I got, let's say, artistically
excited by this actress

01:01:15.290 --> 01:01:19.407
that I think absolutely
has such a strong presence

01:01:19.407 --> 01:01:21.200
and a strength onscreen.

01:01:21.200 --> 01:01:26.200
So I shot more, but it respected
the frame that was written.

01:01:26.680 --> 01:01:28.552
That's what I will say.

01:01:28.552 --> 01:01:30.320
But there's more images.

01:01:30.320 --> 01:01:31.890
There's more in a way

01:01:31.890 --> 01:01:34.743
that the more I was shooting with Zendaya,

01:01:34.743 --> 01:01:35.994
the more I wanted to shoot.

01:01:35.994 --> 01:01:38.427
So that's why I am looking
forward to do part two.

01:01:39.640 --> 01:01:41.070
- Thank you so much.

01:01:41.070 --> 01:01:42.260
- Thank you all so much.

01:01:42.260 --> 01:01:46.150
I think that probably brings
us to an end at exactly 5:01.

01:01:46.150 --> 01:01:47.500
I feel a sense of pride.

01:01:47.500 --> 01:01:48.333
I won't lie.

01:01:48.333 --> 01:01:49.634
- Great job, great job.

01:01:49.634 --> 01:01:51.110
- (laughing) Thank you.

01:01:51.110 --> 01:01:52.235
- Thank you all.

01:01:52.235 --> 01:01:53.068
Thank you, Vanessa.

01:01:53.068 --> 01:01:53.901
Thank you, it was lovely.

01:01:53.901 --> 01:01:54.734
- Thank you so much, everybody.

01:01:54.734 --> 01:01:55.567
- Thank you, Eric and Jon.

01:01:55.567 --> 01:01:56.420
- Thanks so much.
- Thank you.

01:01:56.420 --> 01:01:57.920
- Thank you for participating

01:01:57.920 --> 01:02:01.160
in the Academy's Visiting
Artist Film series

01:02:01.160 --> 01:02:02.953
FilmAid edition for writing.

